            <ArticleTitle>[Efficacy and safety of a very-low-calorie diet in a group of overweight or obese Chilean women].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1286-91</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">/S0034-98872011001000006</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Very low calorie diets (VLCDs) have been used to induce a rapid decrease in total body weight.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To evaluate the efficacy and safety of a VLCD in a group of overweight and obese Chilean women.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIAL AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Thirty women with a body mass index (BMI) &gt; 27 kg/m2 were enrolled to a 4 weeks intervention with a VLCD (using liquid meal replacement). Anthropometric and metabolic parameters were evaluated at baseline and at the end of the intervention.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Twenty five women (83%) completed the total intervention period. Average weight loss was 5.7 ± 1.5 kg (-6.9% of the initial weight) corresponding to a 34.7 ± 13.4% of the excess of weight. Significant improvements in triglycerides, total cholesterol and LDL cholesterol (Low Density Lipoproteins) were observed. Insulin resistance determined by Homeostasis model assessment (HOMA), decreased significantly from 3.3 ± 1.8 to 2.0 ± 0.9 (p = 0.003). No serious adverse events were reported.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Short term use of VLCDs is safe and effective to induce rapid weight loss in Chilean women.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
